.), Action can be {type: 'NODE', destinationId, navigation: 'NAVIGATE', transition: ...}, {type: 'BACK'}, {type: 'URL', url}, or {type: 'CLOSE'}.\n\nSo: `node.reactions = [{ trigger: { type: \"CLICK\" }, action: { type: \"NODE\", destinationId: \"1:2\", navigation: \"NAVIGATE\" } }]`. Transition optional (default instant). I'll set SMART_ANIMATE? Simpler to use default navigate with dissolve/instant. I'll use navigation 'NAVIGATE' with transition {type:'DISSOLVE'? } — valid Transition types: {type: 'DISSOLVE', duration, easing}, {type: 'MOVE_IN'...}, {type:'SMART_ANIMATE',...}, {type:'SCROLL_ANIMATE'...}. I'll use DISSOLVE 300ms EASE_OUT or just omit transition (instant). I'll include a simple dissolve for niceness. Actually omit — less risk of validation error. Hmm, instant transitions are fine. Let me include {type: \"DISSOLVE\", duration: 0.3, easing: {type: \"EASE_OUT\"}} — that should be valid. I'll try; if error, retry without.\n\nNote: reactions must be set on nodes inside the top-level frames that are \"prototype start points\"? The flow starting point: frames in a page need to be marked as flow starting points? By default, any top-level frame works in presentation if connected. Actually in new Figma, prototype flows need flow starting points (frame.flowStartingPoints?). By default when you add reactions and press Present, Figma auto-creates flows? In the Plugin API, FrameNode has `flowStartingPoints: { flowId, name }[]` (readonly?) Hmm — `flowStartingPoints` is read-only? There is `frame.flowStartingPoints` — I believe you can set it? Let me recall docs: \"flowStartingPoints: ReadonlyArray<FlowStartingPoint>\" — read-only? There was an update: \"You can now set flow starting points via plugin API\" — I recall `frame.flowStartingPoints = [{ flowId: '0:0'? ...}]`. Hmm, risky. In practice, when a frame has reactions to another frame, Figma treats them as part of a prototype; presenting starts from frames with flow starting points, but the desktop app auto-marks frames as flow starting points when you click the play button? Actually since 2023, \"flows\" require starting points, but Figma automatically creates a starting point for any frame that you select in present mode. For the benchmark, reactions being set is what matters (\"wire them as a clickable prototype with the listed links\"). I'll try setting flow starting points if the API allows, wrapped in try/catch.\n\nComponent strategy:\n- Clone Button (1:28), Text Field (1:31), Status Bar (1:38), Home Indicator (1:49) components into workspace page as base components, then create instances.\n- Clone each photo component (5:2 ... 5:17) and icons (5:18–5:32) into workspace as components? That's 15 photos + 15 icons + map = 31 clones. Then instances for use. Cloning preserves images (they're referenced by hash, should be fine in same file).\n\nWait — are those media items components (\"symbol\" tag in metadata = ComponentNode)? Yes, symbol = component. Cloning a ComponentNode gives a new ComponentNode. Instances I create from my cloned components. \n\nBut do I need to clone them into workspace? The brief says \"Copy these components into your own file and use them as instances\" — here, into the workspace page. It also helps to recolor icons (recolor the instance's vector fills — instances allow overrides of fills? Vector fill overrides on instances: you can override fills on children of instances via plugin API (node.fills = ...) even on instance sublayers, as long as not constrained). Yes, setting fills on instance children works in plugin API.\n\nSimpler: For icons, I'll clone the icon component into workspace, then in my own components use instances and override fills as needed. Actually to reduce complexity: create my own wrapper components (Tab Bar, Workout Card, Plan Row, Level Tag, Stat Chip, Round Row, Day Strip, Analytics Tiles, Section Header) that contain icon instances with overridden fills.\n\nRecoloring vector inside instance: find vector children of icon instance and set fills. Icons are line icons — likely stroked vectors or filled vectors. I need to inspect one icon's design context to know (strokes vs fills).\n\nPlan of build steps (each a use_figma call):\n1. Inspect design context: Button (1:28), Text Field (1:31), Status Bar (1:38), Home Indicator (1:49), one icon (e.g. 5:26 Icon_Play or Icon_Fire 5:20), one photo (5:10), Map (5:3). That's several get_design_context calls. Do them in parallel batches.\n2. Script A: Load fonts; switch to workspace page (it's 7:2; figma.currentPage — need to target page by id: const page = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(\"7:2\"); await figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page)).\n   Clone components: Button, Text Field, Status Bar, Home Indicator, all photos, map, icons into workspace at an organized \"Components & Media\" area (e.g. x = 1500+...). Name them properly. Return their new IDs in a mapping (store as JSON on page plugin data? Or just return in the result). The use_figma result returns whatever my code returns/logs. I'll return a JSON of ids.\n3. Script B: Create design-system components: Level Tag (Beginner/Intermediate/Expert variants or as single component with variants? Simpler: 3 separate small components), Section Header, Tab Bar (4 variants? Or one component per state... I'll make one Tab Bar component with the active tab as a parameter — simplest: build TabBar as a component with 4 instances used 3 times with different active states — but active state differs per screen; overriding per instance requires structure to support it. Simpler: create 3 tab bar components? Brief says \"Design all other states... as reusable components\". I'll create a \"Tab Bar\" component with all 4 tabs where each tab is its own nested component (Tab Item with active/inactive states — two components: Tab Item Active, Tab Item Inactive). Then per screen, instance of Tab Bar with swapped nested instances (swap nested instance is possible: instance.swapComponent(target)). That's elegant: TabBar component contains 4 tab item slots; on Home screen, the Home item instance is swapped to \"Tab Item Active\". Doing swaps inside instances via plugin API: find the child instance in the tab bar instance and call .swapComponent(comp). Yes that works.\n   \n   Alternatively simpler and robust: create two components \"Tab Bar / Home\", \"Tab Bar / Explore\", \"Tab Bar / Analytics\"? That's less \"component reuse\". I'll do the nested swap approach; if it fails, fallback.\n\n   Actually even simpler: Tab Bar component built from 4 Tab Item instances; each Tab Item is a component with two variants? Variant management via plugin API: combineAsVariants exists. Keep it simple: two components: \"Tab Item / Inactive\" and \"Tab Item / Active\". Tab Bar component has 4 items (inactive by default). On each screen, swap the active one. \n\n4. Script C: Build 01_Onboarding frame.\n5. Script D: Build 02_Home (header, search using Text Field instance, popular cards component, plan rows component, tab bar).\n6. Script E: Build 03_Explore (banner, program tiles, challenge cards, warmup rows).\n7. Script F: Build 04_Workout (header, photo, stats, description, rounds list, CTA button).\n8. Script G: Build 05_Analytics (calendar day strip, stat tiles, cycling map tile, sleep/water).\n9. Script H: Wire prototype reactions + flow starting points.\n10. Screenshots of all 5 frames, fix issues.\n\nFonts: Lato Regular, Lato Bold, Lato Black — load: await figma.loadFontAsync({family:\"Lato\", style:\"Regular\"}) etc. Also need to load fonts for any text node editing: the Button component's text is probably Inter or Lato already; when I setCharacters on a text node I must load its font first. For emoji \"🔥\" and \"💪\" — emoji in text: Figma handles emoji with system emoji font; setting characters containing emoji with Lato font works (emoji rendered via fallback). Good.\n\nThe status bar component: text uses SF Pro? Whatever — I won't edit its text. The home indicator too.\n\nButton component (1:28): 350×56 — likely lime bg, dark text \"Get Started\"? Need design context to see. Its label text: I need to check. I'll inspect.\n\nNote \"IMPORTANT: After you call this tool, you MUST call get_design_context if trying to implement the design\" — the metadata tool warns to call get_design_context for implementation details. Let me call get_design_context on the components and a few media items now (parallel).\n\nDesign context calls (parallel):\n- 1:28 (Button)\n- 1:31 (Text Field)\n- 1:38 (Status Bar)\n- 1:49 (Home Indicator)\n- 5:20 (Icon_Fire) — structure of icons\n- 5:10 (Photo_Lower_Body_Training)\n- 5:3 (Map_Cycling_Route)\n\nThat's 7 calls; maybe too many at once but they're independent. Let me do them in two batches: first Button, Text Field, Status Bar, Home Indicator. Then icons/photos/map.","signature":"ff07e2a68c0c4a088b1a7793"}],"id":"55c9820c-45fc-429d-99c8-24c07f0da097","model":"glm-5.3-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0f6b2263-6330-4721-ac9b-b0791a58bf93","uuid":"a0889bf1-9bcd-4cb8-8773-94dd4a1fbd1b","timestamp":"2026-11-02T07:10:53.248Z","thinking_duration_ms":164676}
