s on the instance's child items via code. Let me just try in script A/B; if throws, use hotspots. I'll write a try/catch helper: setReaction(node, dest) — try node.reactions = [...]; catch → create hotspot. For simplicity and determinism, I'll go hotspots for tab items, and direct reactions for cards/buttons/backs (top-level or simple instances: Button instance reaction — instance is a node, .reactions works; Workout Card instance — I'll instance my WorkoutCard component; reaction on instance OK. But nested play button inside? The whole card → 04 is the required link. Also back button (my Nav Header component instance — reaction needs to be on the back-circle inside instance... use hotspot over it, or set reaction on nested child). I'll use hotspots for anything nested. Direct .reactions on instance root for cards/CTA/GetStarted.\n\n  Hmm wait, actually for the Nav Header back: simpler to make Nav Header component contain title only, and place back button as separate \"Icon Button\" component instance on each screen — reaction directly on the instance. Icon Button (40×40 circle) reusable. Good.\n  \n  Similarly Get Started button = Button component instance → direct reaction. Workout card → instance → direct. CTA Let's Workout → Button instance (lime? or charcoal default — brief: action exists; no link required from it! \"Let's Workout\" is listed as entry-only... \"Entry only (no frame): ... Let's Workout\" means no prototype target needed. Good, but pressed state? \"All states: ... pressed, playing...\" — states listed as mine to design. Adding a pressed variant of Button component? Nice-to-have; I'll add Button/Pressed variant component (lime? pressed = darker lime). Low cost: define second component \"Button / Pressed\" and maybe use as the CTA visual (as if pressed?) No — pressed states usually shown in component sheet, not in flow. I'll create Button/Pressed component in components area for state design credit, not used in screens. Also \"playing\" state: play